# id: BM113-bound-widening-compares-internal-keys
# blinds: a table's bounds being statements about USER KEYS
# covering-lane: cpp-test
# expect: killed
# control-lane: cpp-build
# expect-control: alive
#
# BUG-006, PRESERVED AS A CLASS. The internal order is user key ascending and
# TAG DESCENDING, so at one user key a SMALLER TAG SORTS LATER -- and a
# candidate equal in user key compares "greater" or "lesser" purely by its
# sequence.
#
# THE CONSEQUENCE IS A PERMANENT REFUSAL, NOT DATA LOSS. The manifest records
# one bound, `VerifyTables` derives another, and the Open fails forever after:
# the bytes are intact and the database can never be opened again.
#
# It is planted at the SHARED PREDICATE rather than at either caller, which is
# the fix's whole shape: one implementation of one rule, so the writer and the
# classifier cannot disagree. A mutant here changes both at once -- which is
# exactly what makes the single predicate safe and is why it is worth asserting
# that the pair still moves together.
--- a/engine-cpp/src/internal_key.h
+++ b/engine-cpp/src/internal_key.h
@@ -92,7 +92,8 @@
 // sides diverged.
 inline bool WidensUpperBound(Slice candidate_user_key, Slice current_bound) {
   if (current_bound.size() < kTagBytes) return true;  // no bound yet
-  return candidate_user_key.compare(ExtractUserKey(current_bound)) > 0;
+  // BM113: compared as internal keys, which is what BUG-006 was.
+  return candidate_user_key.compare(current_bound) > 0;
 }
